  "solution": "### 💡 Intuition & Approach\nThe implementation computes **VGG Convolution Block** by following direct mathematical principles, leveraging vectorized operations to ensure both correctness and optimal runtime efficiency.\n\n### ⚡ Python Reference Implementation\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef dl_vgg_block(image: list, conv1_w: list, conv2_w: list) -> list:\n    c1 = np.maximum(0.0, np.array([[np.sum(np.array(image)[i:i+3, j:j+3]*np.array(conv1_w)) for j in range(len(image[0])-2)] for i in range(len(image)-2)]))\n    c2 = np.maximum(0.0, np.array([[np.sum(c1[i:i+3, j:j+3]*np.array(conv2_w)) for j in range(c1.shape[1]-2)] for i in range(c1.shape[0]-2)]))\n    return np.round(np.array([[np.max(c2[2*i:2*i+2, 2*j:2*j+2]) for j in range(c2.shape[1]//2)] for i in range(c2.shape[0]//2)]), 4).tolist()\n```\n\n### ⏱️ Complexity Analysis\n- **Time Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ where $N$ represents the size of the primary input.\n- **Space Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ auxiliary memory for computing and storing output representations.",
